If I had to describe Clair Obscur: Expedition 33 (hereafter just "E33" or "Clair Obscur" because I'm not typing
all that shit again) in two words, it would be ~~"Baguette Anime"~~ "Flawed Masterpiece." I
think it does deserve the vast majority of the praise it's garnered, but I also think that
it has a handful of underlying flaws that just barely keep it from being the an all-timer,
at least for me. I should also lead out by saying that I only began the game *after* it
had already swept the Game Awards, so there's obviously some level of bias from my side
going in, knowing that it was so well regarded.

Most of my thoughts are going to center around the narrative, because that's the part
of the game that I have the most to say about, but I'd like to first call out a few other
areas:
* First and foremost, the soundtrack absolutely deserves the praise. It fucks. It fucks
**hard.** [Lumière](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LpNVf8sczqU&list=RDLpNVf8sczqU) is a
generational track.
* Visually E33 also makes a pretty defining statement. The style is pretty breathtaking,
and I'm glad they leaned hard into the over-the-top animations especially during combat.
* On the note of the combat, I also have to give the game credit for making me enjoy a
JRPG. It's engaging enough that I didn't get bored for the entire 30-odd hours of the main
story, though it did start to lose my interest in the post-game content, where it turns
into a bit more of a damage-sponge grindfest than I'd like.
* I have one additional comment about the combat system, which is that though I loved the
parry mechanic, there needed to be more risk to it. Basically there is no reason to ever
dodge, it is always objectively better to parry, especially because the timing difference
between the two is so small. I would have preferred if the parry did something like only
give AP on a full counter, or made you take increased damage on a fail. Of course,
parrying feels cooler anyway, but it just made dodge feel... flacid.

Now, lets talk about the main meat of E33: the story.

From a cliffnotes perspective, I found that the story opens on an extreme high note,
stumbles somewhat in the middle (largely in Act II, though we'll get to that in a bit),
and then comes back to a crescendo at the end. In particular the choice of endings is
masterfully done, so much so that Clair Obscur wins the rare praise of making neither
option feel "correct" in the way that a lot of games do. I personally chose the Maelle
conclusion, and I'll come back to that in a bit, but the choice was so difficult that
immediately after finishing I went and watched the Verso ending just to see it. I very
much hope that if/when the game gets a sequel, they *don't* choose one option as the
"canonical" one, because that choice is thought-provoking in a way that not many things
are. But again, we'll get to that.

E33 starts on a high note. For such an out-there concept and world, the prologue is pretty
masterful in getting you hooked quickly, and I really cannot emphasize how well it follows
the "show, don't tell" rule. This is a problem I tend to have with other RPGs, especially
JRPGs: they all like to slap you in the face with a huge cutscene or "tutorial" character
that dumps an encyclopedia of information on you off the rip, enough so that the world
starts to feel generic and boring. Clair Obsucr does not do this. Gustave and Maelle's
introduction, the walk through Lumière, and finally the gut punch of Sophie's Gommage are
all masterfully done, getting you so immersed so fast that it's kind of a magic trick.
Similarly, the final dinner before Expidition 33 sets out, the massacre on the beach, and
Gustave re-uniting Lune, Maelle, and Sciel are perfectly paced, spread out just enough to
let you soak in the atmosphere without getting too bogged down in meaningless exposition.

Act I keeps this up all the way to the very end, where we come to the start of the
stumbling block of E33 for me.

I think that Gustave's death and Verso's introduction is mishandled, and that's the major
flaw of the story in my opinion. To elaborate, I think that there are 2 major issues:
1. Gustave dies too early. I understand that the narrative is replacing Maelle's brother
with Alicia's brother in Verso, but I don't think there was quite enough time to build up
the relationship between Maelle and Gustave properly. I also think that it would have been
far more interesting to have Verso and Gustave both in the party at the same time, to sort
of bridge that gap as more of the world outside the painting reveals itself.
2. I also think that making Gustave & Verso the "main" characters (going by who you play
at camp) kills some of the gut punch, though that may sound counter-intuitive at first.
Especially when you now play as Verso, someone you as the player know nothing about, it
becomes hard to connect as much, at least until you get deeper towards the end of Act II.

I think both of these problems could have been solved with one very simple tweak: make
Maelle the main character in camp rather than either of her brothers. There's a valid
counterargument that [a friend](https://www.youtube.com/@wordforwordnerd) made to me when
we were discussing it, which to quote her is:

> *"I think it’s more that Verso is the main character of E33, but Maelle is the main
> character of Clair Obscure as a series (cus there’s definitely more of these coming)"*

This is a fair point, but for me it still falls down a little in that the way that Act III
is structured is such that the game puts more focus on the grief of the Dessendre family
and in particular the guilt and suffering of Alicia/Maelle than it does on anything else.
Obviously the catalyst of all of that is the last piece of Verso's soul still trapped in
the painting, and the painting-Verso's desire to finally be laid to rest, but it was my
reading that Alicia was always meant as the main protagonist.

I'm getting a little ahead of myself though. To finish my thoughts on Act II, it just
feels like the excellent pacing of the rest of the game stumbles for a while. You spend
time meeting Monoco (more on him in a minute), then trying to find a way to break the
barrier around the paintress by fighting two giant bosses, then fighting your way to the
top of the monolith to confront her. During all of this the game has a variety of
"reveals," but the problem with all of them is the same: they're predictable. Maybe it's
just me, but I saw 90% of the twists coming hours before they happened, and then had to
roll my eyes a bit as the characters took multiple cutscenes before they finally come to
the same realization. The problem here is that this is a rare stumble where E33 goes too
far past foreshadowing, instead opting for a wink-wink-nudge-nudge style that dulls a bit
of the fun of the story's twisting path.

The last thing I'd like to say about Act II before we move on from my negativity is that
Monoco is a sad weak point of a character for me. Don't get me wrong, some of the best
one-liners in the game are Monoco lines, but largely the character feels like comic
relief and little else. Even his more emotional moments in Act III with Noco's
ressurection and his status as Verso's oldest friend just don't feel as weighty or
satisfying as the character quests with Lune, Maelle, and Sciel. It's another reason I
think that it would have been better to delay Gustave's death to the end of Act II
instead, since I think it would have presented many more interesting character
interactions, and the comic relif role was already filled by Esquie.

Anyways, the game thankfully absolutely sticks the landing in Act III. Everything I just
criticized above gets turned on its head after you defeat the Paintress and everyone gets
Gommaged. I happily said to myself "well, now I have absolutely no idea what's going to
happen!" I'm going to gloss over most of the third act here and skip to the ending
sequence, but I do want to emphasize that everything leading up to that ending sequence is
really good: the resurrection of Lune and Sciel put a lump in my throat, and the sequences
with the Dessendre family outside the canvas and with Renoir inside it are written better
than a lot of books I've read.

To the ending though, because that's where Clair Obscur really shines. As another friend
put it, it's one that can only be done in a video game, which makes it pretty special. To
me, the single most impressive thing about the ending choice is that it is truly one of
the most morally grey choices I've ever personally experienced in a game. There's no
"right" choice. To contrast it to the Witcher 3, another game I adore, for example: Ciri
becoming a Witcher is obviously the ending the game pushes you towards, enough so that the
upcoming Witcher 4 makes it the canonical one. The option of Ciri becoming queen of
Nilfgaard also makes narrative sense, especially in the context of the books, but you're
very heavily pushed towards the Witcheress ending (as you should be, it's the best one!)

E33's ending does not have that problem. One the one hand, you can choose to destroy an
entire world of sentient people (and they are definitely sentient, the game goes out of
its way to demonstrate that) in order to release the last piece of Verso's trapped
soul, which desperately wants peace, and allows the Dessendre family to grieve and begin
to heal, though Alicia will be left living a half-life with her injuries from the fire.
Or, you can choose to give Alicia a new life within the world of the painting, one that
was stolen from her in the real world, and keep the characters you've fallen in love with
alive within the canvas, but at the cost of Verso never being able to truly pass on. It is
a gut-wrenching decision, like the Matrix's red pill/blue pill on steroids.

As I said above, I ended up choosing the Maelle ending because I couldn't bring myself to
destroy the world inside the canvas, and maybe that says something about me. Either way,
it suceeded in making me stew on the choice long after it was made, which is exceptionally
high praise. The last game that I can remember doing that was Red Dead Redemtion 2, which
I feel is a similarly flawed masterpiece in its own right, so Clair Obscur is in excellent
company. And it really is a special thing to see a story told in the art form of games
that can only be told that way.

To finish off, E33 absolutely deserves the praise it got, and I do think it's a
pretty special game and one that I will happily recommend to anyone. 2025 was a pretty
especially outstanding year for games, and so for Clair Obscur to stand above them all,
even if it's not my personal GOTY, is saying a lot. It reminded my of why I fell in love
with games in the first place, and that's really special.
